.TH AESGCMTEST 1
.SH NAME
aesgcmtest \- test keyring->aesgcmsetup() and the AES-GCM API
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aesgcmtest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Aesgcmtest
checks
.BR aesgcmsetup() ,
.BR aesgcm_setiv() ,
.BR aesgcm_encrypt() ,
and
.B aesgcm_decrypt()
.RI ( module/keyring.m ,
.IR libinterp/keyring.c ,
.IR libsec/aes_gcm.c ,
ported from 9front's own
.B libsec/port/aes_gcm.c
\(em see
.I doc/ssl-modernization-plan.md
in the source tree) \(em primitive 5 of the SSL modernization plan,
needed for TLS 1.2's
.B TLS_ECDHE_RSA_WITH_AES_128_GCM_SHA256
record protection.
.PP
The port drops the source's precomputed GHASH multiplication table
(16*256*4 words, 64KB, per connection) in favour of that same
source's own table-free
.B gfmul()
path \(em already present in the code being ported, normally disabled
behind an
.BR if(0) ,
made live here instead. Correctness is identical; only per-block
speed differs, which does not matter for a client handling a handful
of TLS records.
.PP
Five cases check
.B aesgcm_encrypt()
and
.B aesgcm_decrypt()
together, round-tripping plaintext through both and checking the
ciphertext and tag against known-good vectors: empty plaintext and
AAD; one full 16-byte block with AAD (reusing the classic NIST/
McGrew-Viega GCM test vector's key, IV, AAD and plaintext, truncated
to one block); a 37-byte plaintext (a partial final block) with no
AAD; a 20-byte plaintext (partial final block) with AAD; and a
64-bit \(em i.e. non-96-bit \(em IV, which exercises
.BR aesgcm_setiv() 's
GHASH-based path rather than its fast fixed-counter path for a
96-bit IV. Vectors were generated from Python's own
.B cryptography
library, then cross-checked against
.B pycryptodome
\(em a second, independent implementation (its own C code, not a
wrapper around the same backend) \(em agreeing byte for byte before
being trusted.
.PP
A sixth case flips one byte of a genuine tag and confirms
.B aesgcm_decrypt()
rejects it (via
.IR tsmemcmp (3) ,
already verified constant-time elsewhere in this tree) rather than
silently returning corrupted-looking success.
.PP
A seventh case exercises
.B aesgcm_setiv()
directly \(em the entry point a real TLS record layer calls once per
record on one long-lived state \(em encrypting twice on the same
state with a
.B aesgcm_setiv()
call in between, and confirming the second ciphertext matches what a
fresh IV should produce rather than the first record's keystream
(skipping this call on a real connection is the classic catastrophic
GCM nonce-reuse failure). An eighth confirms
.B aesgcm_setiv()
rejects a zero-length IV.
.PP
Exits with
.B PASS
if every case held, or
.B FAIL
naming which one and what was expected versus computed.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/aesgcmtest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR x25519test (1),
.IR tls12prftest (1),
.IR tlshellotest (1)
.SH BUGS
Does not exercise multi-connection key/IV reuse patterns (a real TLS
1.2 record layer keeps one
.B AESGCMstate
per connection direction across many records, varying only the
explicit per-record nonce via
.BR aesgcm_setiv() )
beyond the one case that calls
.B aesgcmsetup()
twice with the same key/IV to prove decrypt doesn't depend on hidden
state left over from a prior encrypt call.
